        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "You were in hospital after a road traffic collision.\n\nThe team found:\n- A broken left shin bone (closed left tibial shaft fracture)\n- A small tear to your spleen (grade II splenic laceration)\n- Broken ribs on the left, ribs 7 to 9, with a small collection of blood around the lung (haemothorax)\n- Small fractures to a bone in your lower back (undisplaced L1 transverse process fractures)"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "The team did several scans and tests.\n\n- A scan of your abdomen showed the spleen tear was stable.\n- A repeat scan on Day 4 showed the spleen was improving and your blood levels were stable.\n- A chest X-ray on Day 4 was satisfactory after the chest drain was removed.\n\nThe team treated you in these ways:\n\n- On Day 2 (13/05), you had an operation on your left shin bone. A nail was placed inside the bone to hold it (intramedullary nailing of left tibia).\n- The spleen tear was managed without an operation. The team checked your observations and blood levels regularly.\n- A chest drain was put in by the respiratory and thoracics team to treat the blood around your lung. On Day 2 the lung was re-expanding. The drain was removed on Day 4 (15/05).\n- The physiotherapy team helped you to start moving. You are partially weight-bearing on your left leg, as advised by the orthopaedic team."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7"
          ],
          "text": "- You have orthopaedic fracture clinic follow-up at 2 weeks.\n- You have a general surgery telephone review about your spleen at 6 weeks. The team will give you advice about splenic vaccination at that review.\n- The respiratory and thoracics team do not need to see you routinely."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.4",
          "heading": "safety_net",
